-Prediction tasks in healthcare, such as diagnosing a disease or anticipating an adverse outcome, often have to be learned from small datasets, because the disease is rare or because labelling records is expensive. Synthetic data generation can be a solution for such contexts, but established generators, such as oversampling techniques and generative adversarial networks, learn only from the available records, recombining patterns that already exist in the data. Large language models (LLMs) can address this issue by adding domain knowledge acquired during pre-training, without being trained on the target data. When prompted directly, however, LLMs often return fewer records than requested, malformed records or values of the wrong type, so their output cannot be used as a dataset. To address this issue, we present \system, a synthetic data generation system for small healthcare datasets that combines an LLM with a control layer. Inferring the columns and their types from the real data, the control layer checks every response of the LLM, asks for the correction of invalid values and requests new records until the configured number is reached, while recording the cost of every request. In an evaluation on ten public healthcare datasets with LLMs from two providers, \system completed every synthetic dataset to the configured size, equal to the number of real training records, which neither direct prompting of the same LLM, even with its responses forced into a fixed format, nor a curation-based LLM generator achieved, at a small additional cost in tokens. When ten classifiers were trained with and without synthetic records, \system with the strongest LLM gave the largest median F1-score gain among fourteen generators, with losses of at most 2.8 points on any dataset, against up to 10.7 points for oversampling and deep generators; with a weaker LLM, the gains were smaller and less stable.
+Classification tasks in healthcare, such as diagnosing a disease or anticipating an adverse outcome, often have to be learned from small datasets, because the disease is rare or because labelling records is expensive, and synthetic data generation can improve the performance of such classifiers by adding artificial records to the training data. The generators in the literature, however, do not address the difficulties of small data: oversampling techniques and deep generative models learn only from the few available records, recombining patterns that already exist in them, and large language models (LLMs), which add domain knowledge from pre-training, return text that often contains fewer records than requested, malformed records or values of the wrong type. In this context, we developed \system, an LLM-based synthetic data generation system for small healthcare datasets whose control layer infers the columns and types of the real data, checks every response of the LLM, asks for the correction of invalid values and requests new records until the configured number is reached, while recording the cost of every request. \system was tested on ten public healthcare datasets with seven LLMs from two providers and compared with three LLM-based and eight established generators. It was the only LLM-based generator that delivered every synthetic dataset at the configured size, at a small additional cost in tokens, and, with the strongest LLM, it gave the largest median F1-score gain among the fourteen generators.
