+These comparisons isolate what the control layer contributes, because the LLM, the prompts and the context are the same in each pair. Its contribution is reliability, at no cost in utility: with GPT-4o mini on the ten datasets, \system delivered every configured record against 86\% for direct prompting and 78\% for structured outputs, and turned malformed responses into records instead of losing them, while the F1 change of the two did not differ (median difference $+0.08$ pp, Wilcoxon $p=.92$). The gains in utility therefore come from the LLM that the control layer makes usable, which is why they grow with stronger LLMs (Section~\ref{sec:rq4}), and not from the checks themselves, none of whose variants changed utility (below).
+
 The design choices inside the control layer matter much less than the control layer itself (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}). The five repeated runs of the same pipeline differ by up to 0.5 JSD points, 2 correlation points and 0.7 pp of utility, which is the noise floor. Dense context stays inside it; diverse context slightly improves fidelity (JSD 73.27, correlation 73.72) at the cost of a slightly higher attack AUC (0.544); letting the LLM choose its own settings changes nothing but costs 57\% more tokens; and clipping values to the observed ranges lowers JSD by 0.9 points, just beyond the noise floor, without improving utility. The Friedman tests find no difference in utility between the variants ($p=.843$ for accuracy, $p=.872$ for F1). The parameters of Table~\ref{tab:params} behave the same way: changing the context sample, the batch size or the temperature leaves utility within the noise floor ($p=.986$ and $p=.178$), but it changes cost and fidelity. Halving the context sample saves 27\% of the tokens and doubling the batch size 39\%, at slightly lower correlation fidelity, whereas smaller batches cost 73\% more. A temperature of 1.0 is the one change with a large effect: it raises JSD by 3.2 points and correlation fidelity by 8.7 points and makes the records harder to detect (C2ST 0.799 against 0.878), at no cost in utility, privacy or tokens, which suggests that the default of 0.3 is too conservative for fidelity.
 
