-The objective of this work was to determine whether \system makes LLM-based SDG a reliable and useful option for small healthcare datasets. Reliability is achieved, and it is achieved by the control layer: the same LLM, prompted directly, never delivered the configured dataset, whereas \system completed it, recorded its cost and caught the failures of a less disciplined model at almost no extra cost (RQ1). Usefulness is real but bounded and depends on the LLM: the synthetic records preserve the distribution of the real data without revealing real records (RQ2), they help the classifiers that fail on the minority class (RQ3), and with a strong LLM they give the largest median gain among the generators compared, while a weak LLM can lose as much as direct prompting does (RQ4). The alternatives trade one property for another: curation is more stable but discards records, and tree-based generators fit the real distributions more closely by staying close to the real records. No generator, however, improves every dataset, which is consistent with the data-processing inequality~\cite[Sec.~2.8]{cover2006elements}: SDG cannot replace missing patients, and its benefit is bounded by the knowledge the generator brings. In practice, the choice of LLM matters most, temperature is the one parameter that changes fidelity, cost is better measured in tokens than in time (generation time varied by a factor of 1.7 between repeated runs), and the effect of synthetic records should be checked per classifier before they are used.
